• • . • . . • . • . . . • • • . • . • • . • . • . • • . . • . • . . . . . . • . • . . • . . . . . • . . . . • . . . • 4\nL38@P3: Precision and accuracy......................................................... 5\nL39@P3: Results and discussion •....•...•..•..•••..•..•...•......... 0.......... .... ..... 6\nL40@P3: Cobalt chloride.............................................................. 7\nL41@P3: Manganese chloride........................................................... 9\nL42@P3: Nickel chloride •..•••••. G ••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••• \"...... 10\nL43@P3: Summary and conclusions........................................................ 13\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHydrogen Chloride Sparging Crystallization of the Chloride Salts of  (https://stacks.cdc.gov/view/cdc/10528/cdc_10528_DS1.pdf)\nciteturn112view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 24; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://stacks.cdc.gov/view/cdc/10528/cdc_10528_DS1.pdf\",\"pattern\":\"tetrahydrate\"}); Total lines: 1292\nL142@P5: respectively, over the temperature range of 20° to 60° C.\nL143@P5: The salted-out chlorides of cobalt and nickel formed the hexahydrates\nL144@P5: and those of manganese formed the tetrahydrate in saturated metal chlo\u0002ride solutions at 20° C and low concentrations of HCI. Increasing the\nL145@P5: temperature or the HCI concentration caused a 108s in waters of hydra\u0002tion. At 60° C, the salted-out crystals of NiCl2 formed the tetrahy\u0002drate in HCI concentrations up to 18 pct and the dihydrate at higher\nL528@P10: with hexahydrate salts, and MnC12 was in\nL529@P10: equilibrium with tetrahydrate salt at 20°\nL530@P10-11: C in water. These chlorides were in equilibrium with dihydrate salts in con\u0002centrated HCI at 60° C. The dehydration\nL531@P11: to dihydrate crystals was not desirable\nL532@P11: because the dihydrates formed very small,\nL533@P11: needlelike crystals that were difficult\nL534@P11: to filter.\nL535@P11: Data for the three systems at the tem\u0002perature ranges investigated are summa\u0002rized in tables A-I to A-9. The informa\u0002tion in each table represents a composite\nL536@P11: of two or three experiments and is dis\u0002cussed in the following sections. From\nL537@P11: these tabulations, the solubility curves\nL538@P11: in figures 2, 6, and 10 and the tieline\nL539@P11: diagrams in figures 3-5, 7-9, and 11-13\nL540@P11: were plotted.\nL541@P11: COBALT CHLORIDE\nL542@P11: CoCI Z concentration and specific grav\u0002ity as functions of HCI concentration at\nL543@P11: 20° C are tabulated in table A-I and\nL544@P11: plotted in figures 2 and 3. The CoCl 2\nL545@P11: solubility curve in figure 2 is almost\nL546@P11: linear with a slope of -1.4 pct CoClz per\nL547@P11: percent HCl up to an HCI concentration of\nL548@P11: 14 pet. There is a solubility minimum\nL549@P11: of 9.4 pct at 21 pct HCI, and a rapid\nL550@P11: increase in solubility to 16.5 pct CoClz\nL551@P11: at 24 pct HCI. This is an invariant\nL552@P11: point where solid CoC12'6H20 and probably\nL553@P11: CoCl z ' 2HzO are in equilibrium with the\nL554@P11: solution, as evidenced by the change in\nL555@P11: the intersection of tieline plots in fig\u0002ure 3 and the change in crystal habit\nL556@P11: from large, red basal tablets to very\nL557@P11: small, blue-violet, elongated prisms.\nL558@P11: The tieline data are not clear on the\nL559@P11: species produced at the break from the\nL560@P11: hexahydrate. The lower hydrate could be\nL561@P11: a tetrahydrate or dihydrate, or it could\nL562@P11: be of the form HCI'CoCI2'7HzO; previously\nL563@P11: published data (1) show a hexahydrate to\nL564@P11: dihydrate transition. A secondary mini\u0002mum of 15.8 pet CoCl2 at 27 pct HCI is\nL565@P11: followed by a solubility increase to 18.1\nL566@P11: pct COCl z at HCI saturation, 31.9 pct.\nL567@P11: Saturation of HCI should be slightly low\u0002er at the location of this experiment,\nL568@P11: owing to the reduced average ambient\nL569@P11: atmospheric pressure of 642 rom of Hg at\nL570@P11: an elevation of 1,402 m above mean sea\nL571@P11: 50\nL572@P11: N\nL573@P11: (.)\nL574@P11: KEY\nL575@P11: \" 20· C\nL576@P11: a 40· C\nL577@P11: 6 60· C\nL578@P11: <3 25\nL579@P11: 20\nL580@P11: 15\nL581@P11: 10 -\nL582@P11: 5\nL583@P11: 0 5 10\nL584@P11: FIGURE 2. - Solubi I ity of CoCI 2 as functions\nL585@P11: of HCI concentration and temperature in the sys\u0002tem CoCI 2-I-ICI-H 20.\nL586@P11: /-/\\\nL587@P11: !\nL588@P11: 1\\ /\\ KEY /\\\nL589@P11: c 20° C aqueous solubility\nL590@P11: • Wet residue\nL591@P11: • Theoretical intercept\nL592@P11: \\\nL593@P11: \\\nL594@P11: FIGURE 3. - Tieline plot of the data for the\nL595@P11: CoCI 2-HCI-H 20 system at 200 C.\nL596@P11: 7\nL597@P11: level, as compared with most sea level\nL598@P11: locations with an average ambient atmos\u0002pheric pressure of 760 rom Hg.\nL599@P11: No previously published values are\nL600@P11: available for the CoC1 2-HCl-H 20 system at\nL601@P11: 20° C, except the saturation point in\nL602@P11-12: water, which is reported as 34.6 pct 8\nL603@P12: by Linke-Seidell (1). This is close to\nL604@P12: the 33.9-pct solubility determined in\nL605@P12: this study. The CoCI 2-HCI-H20 system was\nL606@P12: studied at 0° C (4) and at 25° C (3).\nL607@P12: The CoCl 2 solubility curve in this re\u0002search is similar in shape to the curves\nL608@P12: of Foote (4) and Bassett and Croucher\nL609@P12: (3); the point of solid-phase transition\nL610@P12: from the hexyhydrate to the dihydrate is\nL611@P12: intermediate in position but closer to\nL612@P12: the 25° C curve. Linke-Seidell (1) in\u0002cluded 0° C data from Engel (2) which are\nL613@P12: considerably different from -Bureau and\nL614@P12: Foote's research. This discrepancy is\nL615@P12: probably due to typographic errors either\nL616@P12: in Engel's original publication or in\nL617@P12: Linke-Seidell, for when Engel's data are\nL618@P12: multiplied by 10, they match Foote's\nL619@P12: data.\nL620@P12: CoCl 2 concentration and specific grav\u0002ity as functions of HCI concentration at\nL621@P12: 40° C are tabulated in table A-2 and\nL622@P12: plotted in figures 2 and 4. The CoCl2\nL623@P12: solubility curve for 40° C is almost\nL624@P12: parallel to the 20° C curve and has the\nL625@P12: same configuration. The solubility of\nL626@P12: CoCl2 in water was 40.9 pct as compared\nL627@P12: to 41.0 pct in Linke-Seidell. The ini\u0002tial slope was -1.3 pct CoCl 2 per percent\nL628@P12: HCI. There was a distinct break in the\nL629@P12: 40° C curve in figure 2 at a solution\nL630@P12: composition of 14.3 pct HCI and 27.8 pct\nL631@P12: COCI2_ This coincided with a jump in the\nL632@P12: tieline data in figure 4 from a H20-CoCI 2\nL633@P12: baseline intercept indicating hexahydrate\nL634@P12: to one indicating tetrahydrate. This co\u0002incided with a distinct change in crystal\nL635@P12: habit from large, red basal tablets to\nL636@P12: very small, blue-violet elongated prisms.\nL637@P12: ° 40· C aqueous solubility\nL638@P12: • Wet residue\nL639@P12: • Theoretical Intercept\nL640@P12: \\\nL641@P12: FIGURE 4. - Tiel ine plot of the data for the\nL642@P12: CoCI 2-HCI.H 20 system at 400 C.\nL643@P12: A further transition from the tetrahy\u0002drate to the dihydrate is indicated in\nL644@P12: figure 4 at a solution composition of\nL645@P12: 17.7 pct HCI and 23.8 pct CoCI2, but no\nL646@P12: corresponding invariant point is apparent\nL647@P12: in figure 2. Since the tieline data are\nL648@P12: not as accurate as the solution composi\u0002tion data, additional experimentation is\nL649@P12: needed to prove or disprove the existence\nL699@P13: transition from MnC12°4H20 to MnCI2·2H20.\nL700@P13: This conclusion is supported by the tie\u0002lines in figure 7 and by the observation\nL701@P13: of a change from fairly large, pink,\nL702@P13: basal tablets to very fine, pink elon\u0002gated prisms. The solubility and solid\nL703@P13: phase data agree with those from equilib\u0002rium tests made by Balarew (6) at 25° C\nL704@P13: that show almost parallel, but slightly\nL705@P13: higher, curves with an invariant point at\nL706@P13: 1S.3 pet MnCI 2-22.7 pct HCI, and wet res\u0002idue tieline plots indicating the transi\u0002tion from MnClzo4H20 to MnCI2·2H20. The\nL707@P13: aqueous solubility information in Linke\u0002Seidell shows a solid-phase transition\nL708@P13: from tetrahydrate to anhydrous at 60° C,\nL709@P13: whereas thermal decomposition data in\nL710@P13: Colton and Canterford (12) indicate the\nL711@P13: follOWing transitions: --\nL712@P13: 50° C 1350 C\nL713@P13: MnC12'4H20 + MnC12'2H20 + MnC1 2 oH2 0 •\nL714@P13: MnCl 2 concentration and specific grav\u0002ity as functions of HCI concentration\nL715@P13: at 40° C are tabulated in table A-5 and\nL716@P13: plotted in figures 6 and S. MnCl 2 sol\u0002ubility was 46.7 pet compared to 47.0\nL717@P13: pct in Linke-Seidell. Crystallization by\nL718@P13: 9\nL719@P13: sparging with HCI decreased the solubil\u0002ity of MnCl 2 to a minimum of 14.4 pct\nL720@P13: at HCI saturation of 30.9 pct. The ini\u0002tial slope of -1.4 pct MnC1 2 per percent\nL721@P13: HCI was parallel to the 20° and 60° C\nL722@P13: curves. There was a possible small in\u0002flection point at 2S.2 pet MnCI 2-IS.S pct\nL723@P13: HCI that marked the transition from MnCl 2\nL724@P13: '4H20 to MnCl2'2H20 in the salted-out\nL725@P13: crystals. Tieline data in figure S show\nL726@P13: the breakpoint at the same place. Visual\nL727@P13: 55\nL728@P13: KEY\nL729@P13: o 20° C\nL730@P13: ° 40° C\nL731@P13: \" 60° C\nL732@P13: L'lI Linke-Seidel\nL733@P13: solubility dotum\nL734@P13: 't 35 a.\nL735@P13: N\nL736@P13: (.) i 30\nL737@P13: 25\nL738@P13: 20\nL739@P13: 15:\nL740@P13: lol~_.':-I __ ..':-1 --,,=1 --=\",1 c--_-:-'-I=--_='I-,--_-::'I\nL741@P13: o 5 10 15 20 25 30 35\nL742@P13: Hel, pef\nL743@P13: FIGURE 6. - SolubIlity of MnCI 2 as functions\nL744@P13: of HCI concentration and temperature in the sys\u0002tem MnCI 2-HCI-H 20.\nL745@P13: KEY \\\nL746@P13: u 20\" C aqueous solubility \\\nL747@P13: • Wet residue t\nL748@P13: <:J.\nL749@P13: ~ • Theorellcal intercept\nL750@P13: ~ ...\nL751@P13: \\\nL752@P13: FIGURE 7. - Tieline plot of the data for the\nL753@P13-14: MnCI 2-HCI.H 20 system at 20° C. 10\nL754@P14: ° 40° C aqueous solubility\nL755@P14: • Wet residue\nL756@P14: • Theoretical intercept\nL757@P14: \\\nL758@P14: \\i \\I\nL759@P14: FIGURE 8 •• Tieline prot of the data for the\nL760@P14: MnCI 2-HCI-H 20 system at 40° C.\nL761@P14: observation also showed a change from\nL762@P14: large basal tablets to very fine elon\u0002gated prisms. This experiment was com\u0002pared with conventional equilibrium ex\u0002perimentation at 40° C by Balarew (~).\nL763@P14: The agreement .in the two sets of data was\nL764@P14: within 0.5 pct. Balarew found that the\nL765@P14: transition from tetrahydrate to dihydrate\nL766@P14: was at 29.1 pct MnC12-16.2 pct HCl. HCl\nL767@P14: saturation was determined to be 30.9 pct,\nL768@P14: compared with 25.3 pct by Balarew.\nL769@P14: MnCl 2 concentration and specific grav\u0002ity as functions of HCl concentration\nL770@P14: at 60° C are tabulated in table A-6 and\nL771@P14: plotted in figures 6 and 9. The 60° C\nL772@P14: curve starts with aqueous solubility\nL773@P14: of 52.1 pct (same value as in Linke\u0002Seidell), The 60° C curve has no breaks\nL774@P14: and slopes at -1.4 pct MnCl 2 per percent\nL775@P14: HCI, The tieline data in figure 9 show a\nL776@P14: single convergence at a composition of\nL777@P14: MnC12'2H20. Microscopic examination of\nL778@P14: the crystals showed very small elongated\nL779@P14: prisms, which were expected because de\u0002composition data in Colton and Canterford\nL780@P14: indicated that the solid phase at 60° C\nL781@P14: was dihydrate. Linke-Seidell found anhy\u0002drous MnC1 2 at 60° C and a transition to\nL782@P14: dihydrate at 146° C; these conditions are\nL783@P14: not likely, The minimum MnCl z solubility\nL784@P14: occurred at MnCl2 concentration of 19.5\nL785@P14: pct and HCI saturation of 25.5 pet.\nL786@P14: NICKEL CHLORIDE\nL787@P14: Data for the system N1CI 2-HCI-HzO at\nL843@P15: Archakova are in approximate agreement\nL844@P15: with figure 10, while the data of Balarew\nL845@P15: show a break at higher NiCl2 and lower\nL846@P15: HCI concentration, as would be expected\nL847@P15: for a higher temperature. Both groups\nL848@P15: of researchers attributed the break to\nL849@P15: the invariant point corresponding to the\nL850@P15: phase change from NiCl z \"6HzO to NiCl 2\nL851@P15: ·4HzO. The tieline data in figure 11\nL852@P15: do not show a clear-cut transition. A\nL853@P15: change of phase is indicated at 8.2 pct\nL854@P15: NiClz-25.1 pct HCI, but the tieline\nL855@P15: 11\nL856@P15: intersection is not precise and the loca\u0002tion is intermediate to the hexahydrate\nL857@P15: and tetrahydrates. Microscopic examina\u0002tion showed that the crystals were green,\nL858@P15: basal tablets with a very large 2V and an\nL859@P15: index of refraction slightly less than\nL860@P15: 1.600 and verified that the crystals pre\u0002cipitated in 0 to 25.1 pct HCI concentra\u0002tion were the hexahydrate. The crystals\nL861@P15: produced at HCI concentrations greater\nL862@P15: than 25.1 pet were yellow-green, more\nL863@P15: platey than prismatic, but not well de\u0002fined. The crystals had a 2V+ of 45 to\nL864@P15: 52 and an index of refraction of 1.600.\nL865@P15: Therefore, it is likely that the break\u0002point at 25.1 pct HCI concentration rep\u0002resents the phase transition of NiCl z\nL866@P15: '6R20 to NiCl z ·4RzO.\nL867@P15: The crystals obtained at 1.1 pct NiCl z\u000237.5 pct RCI concentrations were orthor\u0002hombic, elongated prisms, yellow, very\nL868@P15: small, and difficult to filter, and had\nL869@P15: indices of refraction slightly greater\nL870@P15: than 1.600. These crystals were probably\nL871@P15: dihydrate, even though the tieline data\nL872@P15: showed that the composition was tetrahy\u0002drate, and there were no obvious breaks\nL873@P15: in the solubility curve. Babaeva and\nL874@P15: Archakova did not observe a tetrahydrate\u0002to-dihydrate transition because the maxi\u0002mum RCI concentration was 30.7 pet.\nL875@P15: Balarew found a tetrahydrate-to-dihydrate\nL876@P15: phase change at 25° C, 4.0 pct NiCI2, and\nL877@P15: 29.6 pct HCI. The transition occurred at\nL878@P15: a HCI concentration of 6 to 8 pct less\nL879@P15: than noted in this work and is reason\u0002able because of the 5° C temperature\nL880@P15: difference.\nL881@P15: More research needs to be done on the\nL882@P15: NiCI2-RCI-HzO system to resolve experi\u0002mental uncertainties and to reconcile\nL883@P15: contradictions in the literature. The\nL884@P15: aqueous solubility data in Linke-Seidell\nL885@P15: show\nL886@P15: 28.S0 C 64.3° C\nL887@P15-16: NiC1 2 '6H20 + NiC1 2 04H20 + NiC1 2 0 2H 20, r\nL888@P16: i\nL889@P16: I\nL890@P16: I,\nL891@P16: Ii\nL892@P16: 12\nL893@P16: while the data of Babaeva and Archakova\nL894@P16: for 80° C in Linke-Seidell show a solid\nL895@P16: phase throughout of NiCI2·4H20. The re\u0002sults of Foote (4) shown in Linke-Seidell\nL896@P16: that were supposed to be determined at\nL897@P16: 20° C were actually determined at 0° C\nL898@P16: and show phase changes from hexahydrate\nL899@P16: to tetrahydrate at 4.6 pct NICl2-26.0 pct\nL900@P16: HCI and from tetrahydrate to dihydrate at\nL901@P16: 1.4 pct NiCI 2-35.0 pct HCI. These are\nL902@P16: very close to the phase change composi\u0002tions reported in this paper, even though\nL903@P16: there was a 20° C temperature difference.\nL904@P16: Data for the system NiCI 2-HCI-H20 at\nL905@P16: 40° C were tabulated in table A-8 and\nL906@P16: plotted in figures 10 and 12. Solubility\nL907@P16: in aqueous solution was 43.3 pct, al\u0002though the best fit to the data points up\nL908@P16: to 12.5 pct HCI concentration in figure\nL909@P16: 10 indicates a zero-HCI intercept at 42.4\nL910@P16: pet NiCl z• No data for aqueous solubil\u0002ity at 40° C were given in Linke-Seidell,\nL911@P16: but when data for other temperatures were\nL912@P16: plotted, the solubility curve intersected\nL913@P16: the 40° C projection at 42.3 pct NiC12'\nL914@P16: Solubility at low HCl concentrations de\u0002creased at a slope of -1.5 pct NiCl 2 per\nL915@P16: percent HCI and was almost the same as\nL916@P16: the 20° and 60° C curves. There were no\nL917@P16: breaks in the solubility curve. Minimum\nL918@P16: NiC1 2 solubility was 1.0 pct and occurred\nL919@P16: at HCI saturation of 37.3 pct. The crys\u0002tals were yellow-green, poorly defined,\nL920@P16: and more platey than prismatic, and had a\nL921@P16: 2V+ of 45 to 52 and an index of refrac\u0002tion of 1.600. The tieline data in fig\u0002ure 12 intersect at a point on the base\u0002line corresponding to NiC1 2·4H20 and\nL922@P16: agree with aqueous solubility data in\nL923@P16: Linke-Seidell which show a tetrahydrate\nL924@P16: solid phase at 28.8° to 63.4° C.\nL925@P16: KEY\nL926@P16: o 40· C aqueous solubllity_\nL927@P16: • Wet residue ~.\nL928@P16: • Theoretical Intercept 0-\nL929@P16: ~ .()\nL930@P16: \"-\"\nL931@P16: \\\nL932@P16: FIGURE 12. - Tieline plot of the data for the\nL933@P16: NiCI 2-HCI.H 20 system at 400 C.\nL934@P16: Data for the system NiC1 2-HCI-H20 at\nL935@P16: 60° C were tabulated in table A-9 and\nL936@P16: plotted in figures 10 and 13. Nickel\nL937@P16: chloride tetrahydrate salted out of satu\u0002rated aqueous solution and gave an almost\nL938@P16: linear solubility curve with a slope of\nL939@P16: -1.5 pct NiC1 2 per percent HCI over the\nL940@P16: concentration range of ° to 18.6 pct HCI\nL941@P16: (44.4 to 18.6 pct NiCl z). Linke-Seidell\nL942@P16: gives aqueous saturation as 44.8 pct.\nL943@P16: A slight break in the solubility curve\nL944@P16: in figure 10 and a substantial jump in\nL945@P16: the intersections in the tieline curve in\nL946@P16: figure 13 show that there is a phase\nL947@P16: change from NiC12·4H20 to NiClz·2H20 be\u0002fore the solubility curve decreases to a\nL948@P16: minimum NiCl z solubility of 3.8 pct at\nL949@P16: HCI saturation of 31.7 pct. The phase\nL950@P16: change from the tetrahydrate to the di\u0002hydrate would be expected from the solu\u0002bility data in Linke-Seidell, which show\nL951@P16: that there is a phase change from NiCl2\nL952@P16: ·4H20 to NiC12·2H20 at 64.3° C. The\nL953@P16: crystals changed from poorly defined\nL954@P16: yellow-green particles to very small,\nL955@P16: yellow, elongated prisms at the invariant\nL956@P16: point.\nL957@P16: Two lines of evidence show that NiC1 2\nL958@P16: crystals salted out of solution by HCI\nL959@P16: sparging are in equilibrium with NiCl 2 in\nL960@P16: solution. The solid diamonds in figure\nL961@P16: 10 represent solution concentrations of\nL962@P16: HCI and NiCl2 at 60° C for bottle tests\nL963@P16: representing equilibrium conditions. The\nL964@P16: data are very close to the curve con\u0002structed with data from sparging experi\u0002ments. Data from five readings taken 12\nL965@P16: to 24 h after cessation of sparging did\nL966@P16: not differ by more than 0.1 pct from data\nL967@P16: collected during sparging.\nL968@P16: '\" 60· C aqueous solubility\nL969@P16: • Wet residue\nL970@P16: • Theoretical intercept\nL971@P16: 4H20 2H20\nL972@P16: -- H20, pet\nL973@P16: \\\nL974@P16: FIGURE 13. - Tieline plot of the data for the\nL975@P16-17: NiCI 2\"HCI.H 20 system at 600 C. 13\nL976@P17: SUMMARY AND CONCLUSIONS\nL977@P17: Comparison of published and Bureau\nL978@P17: data from equilibrium testing showed that\nL979@P17: HCI sparging experiments gave the same\nL980@P17: results as equilibrium tests for the\nL981@P17: CoCl z-, MnCl z-, and NiCI 2-HCl-HzO sys\u0002tems. The chlorides of Co, Mn, and Ni\nL982@P17: are very soluble in water and have solu\u0002bilities of 33.9, 42.6, and 37.7 pct,\nL983@P17: respectively, at 20° C and of 48.3, 52.1,\nL984@P17: and 44.4 pct, respectively, at 60° C.\nL985@P17: Many of the metal chlorides decrease in\nL986@P17: solubility and salt out of solution owing\nL987@P17: to the common ion effect when sparged\nL988@P17: with HCl gas. NiCl2 and MnC1 2 fall into\nL989@P17: this category, although NiCl 2 is much\nL990@P17: less soluble than MnCl 2 in solutions sat\u0002urated with HCI. Some chlorides, such\nL991@P17: as FeCI 3 , form strong chloride complexes\nL992@P17: and become more soluble with HCI addi\u0002tion. CoCl2 is unusual in being very\nL993@P17: soluble in water and in concentrated HCI\nL994@P17: and minimally soluble at intermediate HCl\nL995@P17: concentrations.\nL996@P17: The solubility decreases of cobalt,\nL997@P17: manganese, and nickel chlorides up to HCI\nL998@P17: concentrations of approximately 15 to 25\nL999@P17: pct were linear and approximately paral\u0002lel, with slopes of -1.3 to -1.5 pct met\u0002al chloride per percent HCl. Because of\nL1000@P17: the almost identical behavior and high\nL1001@P17: solubilities, there is little likelihood\nL1002@P17: that the chlorides of cobalt, manganese,\nL1003@P17: or nickel can be separated from one\nL1004@P17: another by sparging to HCI concentrations\nL1005@P17: of less than 15 to 25 pet. At higher HCI\nL1006@P17: concentrations, the solubilities of the\nL1007@P17: three salts diverge owing to large dif\u0002ferences in the strengths of the chloride\n"
